==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|ONF.|carbuncle}}, from {{der|en|la|carbunculus}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|[ˈkʰɑːbʌŋkl̩]}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|[ˈkʰɑɹbʌŋkl̩]}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} A deep-red or fiery colored [[garnet]] or other dark red [[precious stone]], especially when cut [[cabochon]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1602|author=William Shakespeare|authorlink=William Shakespeare|title=[[s:Hamlet|Hamlet]]|section=Act II, scene 2|line=401|passage=With eyes like '''carbuncles''', the hellish Pyrrhus {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1611|title=[[Bible]] ({{w|King James Version}})|section=[[Isaiah]] 54:12|passage=And I will make thy windows of agates, and thy gates of '''carbuncles''', and all thy borders of pleasant stones.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1634|author=Thomas Herbert|authorlink=Sir Thomas Herbert, 1st Baronet|title=A Relation of Some Yeares Trauaile, Begunne Anno 1626. Into Afrique and the Greater Asia, especially the Territories of the Persian Monarchie: And some Parts of the Orientall Indies, and Iles Adiacent. Of their Religion, Language, Habit, Discent, Ceremonies, and other Matters Concerning Them: Together with the Proceedings and Death of the Three Late Ambassadours: Sir D. C[otton] Sir R. S[herley] and the Persian Nogdi-Beg: As also the Two Great Monarchs, the King of Persia, and the Great Mogol|location=London|publisher=Printed by [[w:William Stansby|William Stansby]], and Iacob Bloome|oclc=644078533|editor2=[[w:William Foster (historiographer)|William Foster]]|title2=Travels in Persia 1627–1629. Abridged and Edited by Sir William Foster {{upright|[...]}} with an Introduction and Notes {{upright|(Broadway Travellers)}}|location2=London|publisher2=[[w:Routledge|G. Routledge & Sons]]|year2=1928|oclc2=4900176|page2=79|passage=His turban, or mandil [''mandīl''], was of finest white silk interwoven with gold, bestudded with pearl[s] and '''carbuncles'''; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1936|author=Rollo Ahmed|title=The Black Art|publisher=Long|location=London|page=155|passage=A piece of marigold or bay leaf was imbedded in the metal, and over it a '''carbuncle''' or chrysolite was placed.}}
# {{lb|en|heraldiccharge}} A [[charge]] or [[bearing]] supposed to represent the precious stone, with eight [[sceptre]]s or [[stave#Noun|staves]] [[radiate|radiating]] from a common centre; an [[escarbuncle]].
# {{lb|en|pathology}} An [[abscess]] larger than a [[boil]], usually with one or more [[opening]]s [[drain#Verb|drain]]ing [[pus]] onto the [[skin]]. It is usually caused by [[bacterial]] [[infection]].
# An [[unpopular]] or [[ugly]] [[building]]; an [[eyesore]].

